# Third-party code reuse in plugin-jetkvm

This repository's plugin is MIT-licensed (see `/LICENSE`). Its device client
under `candy/plugin-jetkvm/internal/kvmclient` is VENDORED — copied and adapted —
from two MIT/BSD-licensed reference implementations of the JetKVM local
control-plane protocol, because neither is importable as a Go module (both keep
their client under a Go `internal/` path, which Go forbids importing across
module boundaries) and the upstream firmware `github.com/jetkvm/kvm` is
GPL-2.0 and therefore cannot be linked into this MIT plugin.

The wire format itself (JSON-RPC method names, the `hidrpc` message-type bytes,
the data-channel labels) is an interface, reimplemented from the public
firmware source; the vendored code is the higher-level client/handshake logic.

## Vendored trees

| Path | Origin | License | Nature |
|---|---|---|---|
| `candy/plugin-jetkvm/internal/kvmclient/` | LeeroyDing/jetkvm-mcp (MIT) | MIT | vendored PRODUCTION client (package renamed `jetkvm` → `kvmclient`) |
| `candy/plugin-jetkvm/internal/kvmclient/hidproto/` | LeeroyDing/jetkvm-mcp (MIT) | MIT | vendored HID wire codec |
| `candy/plugin-jetkvm/internal/fakedevice/` | LeeroyDing/jetkvm-mcp (MIT) | MIT | vendored in-process fake JetKVM device, used only by this project's tests |
| `conallob/mcp-jetkvm` (BSD-3) | reference only | BSD-3 | virtual-media / USB method shapes re-derived, no file copied |

Upstream's own TEST SUITE is deliberately NOT vendored: it is upstream's
coverage of upstream's code, not this change's coverage obligation. The coverage
this repository ships is its own (`candy/plugin-jetkvm/provider_test.go`, driving
the real `plugin_input` → `Invoke` path against the vendored fake device, plus
the live R10 bed in the root `charly.yml`).

The ONLY local modifications to vendored files are: the package rename, the
module import-path rewrite, and one added file
(`internal/kvmclient/charly_extension.go`) holding this project's additions
(`Call`, `ControlLease`, `NormalizeDeviceURL`, `applyInsecureTLS`, and the drag
wrapper). No vendored file bodies were otherwise edited, so a future re-vendor
against a newer upstream revision remains a copy rather than a merge.

